/* book exam traps: Adding harmonic RMS values directly: 70.7 + 21.2 = 91.9 V is wrong, because RMS combines as the root of the sum of squares. | Expecting a third-harmonic current to produce average power with the fundamental voltage. sin(ωt)·sin(3ωt) averages to zero over a period. | Leaving the DC term out of the RMS: Vrms² contains V0², not harmonics alone. | Putting total RMS in the THD denominator. THD is always referred to the fundamental RMS. | Assuming half-wave symmetry keeps even harmonics. It removes them, and the DC, leaving odd orders only. */
